# spectroscopy.cyr — spectroscopy: the Beer–Lambert law, photon energy conversions, the Bohr model and the Rydberg
# formula, vibrational and rotational levels, mass-spectrometry isotope patterns, the FFT magnitude spectrum and peak
# detection (port of rust-old/src/spectroscopy.rs).
#
# SI as Rust documents it: energies in J (eV where a name says so), wavelengths in m, frequencies in Hz, wavenumbers
# in cm⁻¹, masses in any consistent unit (kg for the oscillator and the rotor). PLANCK, SPEED_OF_LIGHT, RYDBERG and
# RYDBERG_EV are src/constants.cyr's KM_* (generated), ELEMENTARY_CHARGE (Rust's electrochemistry::) too; the
# SpectralSeries enum, its n_lower column, the isotope abundance table (ADR 0002 #23: from its cited sources) and Rust's
# private RYDBERG_HYDROGEN (the reduced-mass-corrected R_H, read through the API) are src/spectroscopy_data.cyr
# (generated). Fallible functions
# return a value-form Result whose Err payload is a KM_ERR_* code, and reject NaN and ±∞ inputs where Rust does.
# Every expression keeps Rust's operation order, constant by constant, so the arithmetic functions are
# bit-identical (class B: + − × ÷ and √) but where an ADR 0002 row below says otherwise; transmittance (10^x) and
# absorbance_from_transmittance (log10) are class C on src/mathx.cyr's correctly rounded _km_cr_exp10 / _km_cr_log10,
# and fft_spectrum on its correctly rounded twiddles (_km_cr_sincos), all compared exactly against the corrected oracle
# (docs/development/port-parity.md §15).
#
# - Rust's integer arguments are words, never wrapped (port plan §6.4): a u8 Z or a u32 n / line / count outside its
#   type is Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) at the position of Rust's first check of it (the `== 0` tests: one test
#   `z < 1 || z > 255`), NaN for a bare f64 (harmonic_oscillator_energy, rigid_rotor_energy), the empty slice for
#   isotope_abundances; in isotope_pattern a Z outside 0 ..= 255 is Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_ELEMENT), as an unknown Z
#   (port plan §6.4, M1: an atomic number is never aliased mod 256), and a count outside u32 Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT).
# - SpectralSeries is enum KmSpectralSeries (append-only, Rust's variant order); its methods are
#   spectral_series_n_lower(s) (0 for a word that is no value, which no series has) and spectral_series_wavelength(s,
#   line) (Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) for such a word, checked first).
# - Slices are a pointer and a count; a negative count, or a null pointer with values, is Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT)
#   (every slice function here has an error channel). Rust's Vec results go to the caller's buffer `out` of `cap`
#   elements (a negative cap or a null out holds none): Ok(count), the count written, or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) when
#   the result does not fit, checked after Rust's own errors, with the buffer untouched. isotope_abundances returns its
#   slice as (pointer, count) into the baked table.
# - ADR 0002 #19 (the user's policy of 2026-11-09, case (a): a simple exact constant Rust misrepresents):
#   wavenumber_from_wavelength and wavelength_from_wavenumber return the correctly rounded 1/(100·x)
#   (src/mathx.cyr's _km_cr_hundredth_div), where Rust's `0.01 / x` rounds 1/100 and then the quotient (one ulp off on
#   about 14 % of inputs). The golden rows keep the oracle's values; where the port's differ, the note names them and
#   the harness maps the two (accepted under ADR 2, counted).
# - ADR 0002 #23 (M4e-4, the user's decision of 2026-11-10): the isotope table is the cited AME2020 masses (full
#   precision) and TICE 2009 abundances with ³⁶S, not Rust's five-decimal masses without it; isotope_pattern's algorithm
#   is Rust's unchanged, so only its results move (every pattern with an element that has data). The golden rows keep
#   the oracle's values; their notes name the port's whole result (accepted under ADR 2, counted).
# - isotope_pattern is Rust's pruned convolution operation for operation: the scratch peaks sorted by a stable
#   insertion sort on the masses' IEEE total order (Rust's `sort_by(total_cmp)` is stable, and the binned means depend
#   on the order of equal masses), binning, Rust's `fold(0.0, max)` (NaN-ignoring, src/mathx.cyr's _km_max), the
#   renormalisation, `retain` in order; the count loop runs `count` times, as Rust's. The working pattern and scratch
#   start in two stack tables of _KM_ISO_STACK records and move to two buffers of src/validate.cyr's pool of reusable
#   work tables only past that (2.0.1: 2.0.0's bump allocations were never freed, so each such call grew the heap);
#   an allocation failure is Err(KM_ERR_COMPUTATION_ERROR). Rust's private
#   ISOTOPE_PRUNE_REL (10⁻¹²), ISOTOPE_REPORT_REL (0.001) and ISOTOPE_BIN_WIDTH_U (0.5) are typed formula constants
#   inline in the private helpers that apply them (_km_isotope_normalize, _km_isotope_report, _km_isotope_bin), as
#   nuclear.cyr's tolerance (port plan §6.4): pinned by golden rows where a row reaches them (× ½ and × 2) and by
#   tests/spectroscopy.tcyr on the helpers to the ulp (no molecule puts a peak on a threshold).
# - fft_spectrum is hisab 1.4.0's `num::fft` transcribed (ADR 0003, M4e amendment): its bit-reversal loop, its
#   butterflies, Complex's mul / add / sub and the `w *= wn` recurrence in their operation order, no FMA, and the
#   magnitude sqrt(re·re + im·im) (Complex::abs). The twiddle wn = (cos(−2π/len), sin(−2π/len)) is _km_cr_sincos's,
#   correctly rounded; hisab 3.4.1's num_fft is not used (its twiddles are the stdlib's f64_cos / f64_sin, not
#   correctly rounded, reseeded every 64 steps: other bits from n = 256 on). The n complex values live in `out` when
#   cap ≥ 2n and `signal` lies outside it (no allocation), else in 16·n heap bytes.
# - ADR 0002 #36 and #37 (the overflow audit's B6; the user's decision "FFT: both" — the magnitudes and the
#   butterflies, without the range limit — and find_peaks' exact threshold, settled under the policy): fft_spectrum is
#   #20's semantics over the whole transform — every butterfly and twiddle operation with an unbounded exponent
#   (src/mathx.cyr's _km_ux_*), each magnitude's √ rounded once — behind a per-operation fast test that keeps Rust's
#   bits wherever its transform stays in the normal range (a NaN or ±∞ sample keeps Rust's IEEE spectrum); find_peaks
#   decides w > min·(1 − t) + max·t exactly (src/mathx.cyr's _km_sign_scaled), Rust's rounded threshold deciding
#   wherever a proven bound separates the value from it. The golden rows keep the oracle's values; where the port's
#   differ, the note names its whole result (accepted under ADR 2, counted).
# - ADR 0002 #21 and #22 (M4e-3, the user's decisions of 2026-11-10): bohr_energy_level, bohr_transition_energy,
#   rydberg_wavelength and spectral_series_wavelength return their exact rationals (the constant as its double) rounded
#   once, and reduced_mass the correctly rounded m₁·m₂/(m₁ + m₂) for every pair of positive finite masses (no
#   intermediate over- or underflows): so −R exactly at n = Z, m/2 for a homonuclear diatomic, and no cancellation for
#   close large n (the Bohr model's section below; src/mathx.cyr's _km_cr_ratio, _km_dd_mul and _km_dd_div_cr). The
#   golden rows keep the oracle's values; where the port's differ, the note names them (accepted under ADR 2, counted).
# - ADR 0002 #30 (the overflow audit's B2; the user's decisions of 2026-11-09 and 2026-11-10, "library-wide audit" and
#   "round once"): absorbance's (ε·l)·c, concentration_from_absorbance's A/(ε·l), rigid_rotor_energy's
#   ((h·B)·J)·(J + 1), rotational_constant's h/(((8·π)·π)·I) and diatomic_moment_of_inertia's (μ·r)·r are each one
#   product or quotient of inputs and constants' doubles (h, π as its double each time Rust uses it, 8 and J + 1 exact),
#   so the port returns the exact value rounded once (src/mathx.cyr's correctly rounded monomial: _km_cr_mul3,
#   _km_cr_div2, _km_cr_mul4 and _km_cr_div3), and vibrational_frequency's (k/μ).sqrt() the correctly rounded √ of the
#   exact k/μ (_km_cr_sqrt_div), where Rust rounds two to four times (1–2 ulp off on a fraction of inputs) and a partial
#   product or quotient that over- or underflows gives ±∞, 0, NaN or a degraded value although the result is
#   representable (vibrational_frequency(500, 2⁻¹⁰²²) is ∞ in Rust, 1.50·10¹⁵⁵ here; rigid_rotor_energy(2³² − 1, 10⁻³⁰⁰)
#   0, not 1.22·10⁻³¹⁴). Rust's checks and Err codes are kept; the unvalidated inputs (absorbance's, the moment of
#   inertia's, the rotor's B, concentration_from_absorbance's A) keep Rust's expression where one is not finite.
# - Kept as Rust's (reviewed in M4e, reported to the user): the isotope pattern's thresholds 0.001 and 10⁻¹²
#   (heuristic and numerical cutoffs, compared as Rust does), a chain of rounded operations on inputs which ADR 0002's
#   policy leaves as Rust's sequence. (The FFT's sums and re² + im² and find_peaks' interpolated threshold, on that
#   list until the overflow audit's B6, are ADR 0002 #36 and #37 above.)

include "lib/alloc.cyr"

# The [lib] order lets this module call the private helpers of the modules before it (as nuclear.cyr calls thermo.cyr's
# _km_sum): element.cyr's lookups, and validate.cyr's _km_is_enum_value, _km_is_u8 and _km_is_u32 (organic.cyr's,
# inorganic.cyr's and nuclear.cyr's until 2.0.1).

# The isotope_pattern working tables' stack capacity, in (mass, intensity) records: the pattern and the scratch each.
# A step's scratch is (pattern peaks) × (the element's isotopes): since ADR 0002 #23 sulfur has four, so S₈'s largest is
# 4·17 = 68 records and cystine's 4·11 = 44 (glucose's 3·10 = 30); a molecule past 192 moves both to the heap.
const _KM_ISO_STACK = 192;

# ── Beer–Lambert law ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The Beer–Lambert law A = ε·l·c: the absorbance (dimensionless) from the molar absorptivity ε (L/(mol·cm)), the path
# length l (cm) and the concentration c (mol/L). Inputs are not validated. ADR 0002 #30: the correctly rounded ε·l·c
# for finite inputs (a zero factor gives its signed zero), where Rust's (ε·l)·c rounds twice; a non-finite input gives
# Rust's (ε·l)·c. Mirrors `spectroscopy::absorbance`, with ADR 0002 #30.
#must_use
fn absorbance(molar_absorptivity: f64, path_length: f64, concentration: f64): f64 {
    var f, ok = _km_mul3_fast(molar_absorptivity, path_length, concentration);
    if (ok == 1) { return f; }
    var r: f64 = _km_mul3_rest(molar_absorptivity, path_length, concentration);
    return r;
}

# The transmittance T = 10^(−A) from the absorbance A, as Rust's `10.0_f64.powf(-a)`: the correctly rounded
# _km_cr_exp10(−A) (a correctly rounded pow(10, y) is the same value). Inputs are not validated (NaN gives NaN, A = −∞
# gives +∞). Mirrors `spectroscopy::transmittance`.
#must_use
fn transmittance(absorbance: f64): f64 {
    var na: f64 = -absorbance;
    var t: f64 = _km_cr_exp10(na);
    return t;
}

# The absorbance A = −log₁₀(T) from the transmittance T. Ok(A) (−0.0 for T = 1: −log₁₀ 1 is −0.0, as in Rust), or
# Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if T is not in (0, 1] (NaN included). Mirrors `spectroscopy::absorbance_from_transmittance`.
#must_use
fn absorbance_from_transmittance(transmittance: f64): Result {
    if (_km_is_positive(transmittance) != 1 || transmittance > 1.0) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var l: f64 = _km_cr_log10(transmittance);
    var a: f64 = -l;
    return Ok(a);
}

# The concentration c = A/(ε·l) in mol/L from the absorbance A, the molar absorptivity ε (L/(mol·cm)) and the path
# length l (cm). Ok(c), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if ε, then l, is not positive and finite; A is not validated.
# ADR 0002 #30: the correctly rounded A/(ε·l) for a finite A (A = ±0 gives that signed zero), where Rust's A/(ε·l)
# rounds twice and ε·l can over- or underflow; a non-finite A gives Rust's A/(ε·l). Mirrors
# `spectroscopy::concentration_from_absorbance`, with ADR 0002 #30.
#must_use
fn concentration_from_absorbance(absorbance: f64, molar_absorptivity: f64, path_length: f64): Result {
    if (_km_is_positive(molar_absorptivity) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (_km_is_positive(path_length) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (_km_is_finite(absorbance) != 1) {
        var el: f64 = molar_absorptivity * path_length;
        var c: f64 = absorbance / el;
        return Ok(c);
    }
    var r: f64 = _km_cr_div2(absorbance, molar_absorptivity, path_length);
    return Ok(r);
}

# ── photon energy, wavelength and frequency ─────────────────────────────────────────

# The photon energy E = hν in J from the frequency ν (Hz). Inputs are not validated. Mirrors
# `spectroscopy::photon_energy_from_frequency`.
#must_use
fn photon_energy_from_frequency(frequency_hz: f64): f64 {
    var e: f64 = KM_PLANCK * frequency_hz;
    return e;
}

# The photon energy E = hc/λ in J from the wavelength λ (m), as Rust's (h·c)/λ. Ok(E), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if
# λ is not positive and finite. Mirrors `spectroscopy::photon_energy_from_wavelength`.
#must_use
fn photon_energy_from_wavelength(wavelength_m: f64): Result {
    if (_km_is_positive(wavelength_m) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var hc: f64 = KM_PLANCK * KM_SPEED_OF_LIGHT;
    var e: f64 = hc / wavelength_m;
    return Ok(e);
}

# The wavelength λ = hc/E in m from the photon energy E (J), as Rust's (h·c)/E. Ok(λ), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if
# E is not positive and finite. Mirrors `spectroscopy::wavelength_from_energy`.
#must_use
fn wavelength_from_energy(energy_j: f64): Result {
    if (_km_is_positive(energy_j) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var hc: f64 = KM_PLANCK * KM_SPEED_OF_LIGHT;
    var l: f64 = hc / energy_j;
    return Ok(l);
}

# The frequency ν = c/λ in Hz from the wavelength λ (m). Ok(ν), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if λ is not positive and
# finite. Mirrors `spectroscopy::frequency_from_wavelength`.
#must_use
fn frequency_from_wavelength(wavelength_m: f64): Result {
    if (_km_is_positive(wavelength_m) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var nu: f64 = KM_SPEED_OF_LIGHT / wavelength_m;
    return Ok(nu);
}

# An energy in J as eV: E/e (KM_ELEMENTARY_CHARGE, exact in the 2019 SI). Mirrors `spectroscopy::joules_to_ev`.
#must_use
fn joules_to_ev(energy_j: f64): f64 {
    var e: f64 = energy_j / KM_ELEMENTARY_CHARGE;
    return e;
}

# An energy in eV as J: E·e. Mirrors `spectroscopy::ev_to_joules`.
#must_use
fn ev_to_joules(energy_ev: f64): f64 {
    var e: f64 = energy_ev * KM_ELEMENTARY_CHARGE;
    return e;
}

# ── Bohr model ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ADR 0002 #21 (M4e-3, the user's decision of 2026-11-10): the Bohr levels, the transition energy and the Rydberg
# formula return their exact rational values, with the constant taken as its double (R = KM_RYDBERG_EV, R∞ = KM_RYDBERG,
# R_H = _KM_RYDBERG_HYDROGEN), rounded ONCE to binary64 (nearest, ties to even):
# - bohr_energy_level: −R·Z²/n²; bohr_transition_energy: R·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²)/(n₁²·n₂²);
# - rydberg_wavelength and spectral_series_wavelength: n₁²·n₂²/(R·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²)).
# Rust's ((−R·Z)·Z)/(n·n), E(n₂) − E(n₁) and 1/(((R·Z)·Z)·(1/n₁² − 1/n₂²)) round three to eight times, and the factor
# 1/n₁² − 1/n₂² and the difference of levels cancel for close large n (39 ulp at n = 100, 939 at 10⁴; on the corpus up
# to 9.0·10⁷ ulp for the wavelength and 1.8·10⁹ for the transition energy at n near 2³²). Every value here is normal
# (|E| ≥ R·2⁻⁶⁴, λ ≤ 2⁷²), so no exponent bound intervenes. Each has a fast path and an exact slow path:
# - the fast path evaluates the exact numerator and denominator as double-doubles — exact for n₂ < 2¹³ (R·Z² by
#   _km_rz2, R·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²) a TwoProduct of R and an integer below 2⁴², n² and (n₁·n₂)² integers below 2⁵²), each
#   within 2^-102.99 above (n², n₁·n₂ and (n₂ − n₁)(n₂ + n₁) exact TwoProducts, one src/mathx.cyr _km_dd_mul each) — and
#   rounds their quotient by _km_dd_div_cr (its budget: 13.12u² for the quotient, 32u² for the operands, u = 2^-53; the
#   level's quotient by a 26-bit n² inline, to 5.04u²);
# - the slow path forms the integers p and q in src/mathx.cyr's multi-word arithmetic (R = M·2^E, M < 2^53; p, q below
#   2^134) and rounds p/q·2^E once with _km_cr_ratio, ties exactly.
# The rounding test fails with probability about 2^-46, and on every midpoint: the levels have none (R's odd
# significand is 683·11214237642539, squarefree with a prime above 2³², so −R·Z²/n² is a double exactly when n/Z is a
# power of two, and otherwise has at least 56 significant bits), the Rydberg wavelengths none (R∞'s and R_H's odd
# significands have the primes 1963825758347083 and 3319205424319, above 2³², which no n₁, n₂ < 2³² cancel: λ is never
# dyadic), the transition energies 13 (Z = 255, (n₁, n₂) = 2^k·(58,055, 524,544), k = 0 … 12: scripts/oracle's
# exhaustive search of the domain, which also finds its 5,657 exact values, 16R at (15; 3, 5) among them). The checks
# and their order are Rust's.

# The Bohr energy level of a hydrogen-like atom, E_n = −R∞hc·Z²/n² in eV, with RYDBERG_EV (13.6057 eV: an infinitely
# heavy nucleus), from the nuclear charge Z (Rust's u8, 1 for hydrogen) and the principal quantum number n (Rust's
# u32, ≥ 1). ADR 0002 #21: the correctly rounded −R·Z²/n² (rounded once), so −R exactly whenever n = Z (and −R/4^k for
# n = 2^k·Z), where Rust's ((−R·Z)·Z)/(n·n) rounds three times. Ok(E), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if Z is 0 (or
# outside 0 ..= 255), then if n is 0 (or outside 0 ..= u32::MAX). Mirrors `spectroscopy::bohr_energy_level`, with ADR
# 0002 #21.
#must_use
fn bohr_energy_level(z, n): Result {
    if (z < 1 || z > 255) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (n < 1 || n > 0xFFFFFFFF) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var e, ok = _km_bohr_level_fast(z, n);
    if (ok == 1) {
        var ne: f64 = -e;
        return Ok(ne);
    }
    var a: f64 = _km_bohr_level_acc(z, n);
    var na: f64 = -a;
    return Ok(na);
}

# R·Z²/n² (the level's magnitude) by the fast path: (RN, 1), or (0, 0) when the rounding test fails. The operands carry
# no error: R·Z² = p_h + p_l exactly (_km_rz2), and n² is an exact double. For n < 2¹³ (n² < 2²⁶, at most 26 bits) the
# quotient is inline, with y0·n² = y_h·n² + y_l·n² exact (Veltkamp's split of y0: 26 and 27 bits): n² is exact, so
# p_h − y_h·n² is exact (Sterbenz), (p_h − y_h·n²) − y_l·n² = p_h − y0·n² to u²·p_h, + p_l to 2.01u², so r = N − y0·n²
# to 3.01u²·N; |r| ≤ 2.02u·N, d = RN(r/n²) is r/n² to 2.02u²·v, and y0 + d is v to 5.04u² (u = 2^-53), well inside
# _km_dd_div_cr's test budget, whose test is the same (ε = 2^-100·y0). Above, _km_dd_div_cr with n² as an exact
# TwoProduct (n < 2³²).
#must_use
fn _km_bohr_level_fast(z, n): (f64, i64) {
    var r: f64 = KM_RYDBERG_EV;
    if (n < 0x2000) {
        var z2: f64 = f64_from(z * z);              # _km_rz2, inline
        var sp: f64 = 134217729.0;
        var cr: f64 = sp * r;
        var rh: f64 = cr - (cr - r);
        var rl: f64 = r - rh;
        var a1: f64 = rh * z2;
        var a2: f64 = rl * z2;
        var ph: f64 = a1 + a2;
        var pl: f64 = a2 - (ph - a1);
        var q: f64 = f64_from(n * n);
        var y0: f64 = ph / q;
        var cy: f64 = sp * y0;
        var yh: f64 = cy - (cy - y0);
        var yl: f64 = y0 - yh;
        var s: f64 = ph - (yh * q);
        var s1: f64 = s - (yl * q);
        var rr: f64 = s1 + pl;
        var d: f64 = rr / q;
        var k100: f64 = 0x39B0000000000000;         # 2^-100
        var eps: f64 = y0 * k100;
        var lo: f64 = d - eps;
        var hi: f64 = d + eps;
        var w1: f64 = y0 + lo;
        var w2: f64 = y0 + hi;
        if (w1 != w2) { return (0.0, 0); }
        return (w1, 1);
    }
    var ph2, pl2 = _km_rz2(r, z);
    var nf: f64 = f64_from(n);
    var qh, ql = _km_two_prod(nf, nf);
    var y, ok = _km_dd_div_cr(ph2, pl2, qh, ql);
    return (y, ok);
}

# r·Z² as an exact double-double (h, l), for 1 ≤ Z ≤ 255 and a positive normal r below 2⁹⁰⁰: Veltkamp's split
# r = r_h + r_l (26 and 27 significant bits), r_h·Z² and r_l·Z² exact (Z² < 2¹⁶), their FastTwoSum (|r_h·Z²| ≥
# |r_l·Z²|).
#must_use
fn _km_rz2(r: f64, z): (f64, f64) {
    var z2: f64 = f64_from(z * z);
    var sp: f64 = 134217729.0;
    var cr: f64 = sp * r;
    var rh: f64 = cr - (cr - r);
    var rl: f64 = r - rh;
    var a1: f64 = rh * z2;
    var a2: f64 = rl * z2;
    var h: f64 = a1 + a2;
    var l: f64 = a2 - (h - a1);
    return (h, l);
}

# RN(R·Z²/n²) by the exact path alone: _km_cr_ratio(M·Z², n², E) for R = M·2^E.
#must_use
fn _km_bohr_level_acc(z, n): f64 {
    var rr: f64 = KM_RYDBERG_EV;
    var rm, re = _km_f64_int(_km_bits(rr));
    var p[64];
    var q[64];
    _km_bn_set(&p, rm);
    _km_bn_mul_small(&p, z * z);
    _km_bn_set(&q, n);
    _km_bn_mul_small(&q, n);
    var v: f64 = _km_cr_ratio(&p, &q, re);
    return v;
}

# The energy in eV of the photon emitted in the transition n_upper → n_lower of a hydrogen-like atom (Bohr model, an
# infinitely heavy nucleus): E(n_upper) − E(n_lower), positive. ADR 0002 #21: the correctly rounded
# R·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²)/(n₁²·n₂²) (rounded once), where Rust subtracts two rounded levels (which cancel for close large n).
# Ok(E), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) from bohr_energy_level(Z, n_lower)'s checks (Z, then n_lower), then
# bohr_energy_level(Z, n_upper)'s (n_upper), then if n_upper ≤ n_lower (Rust's order). Mirrors
# `spectroscopy::bohr_transition_energy`, with ADR 0002 #21.
#must_use
fn bohr_transition_energy(z, n_lower, n_upper): Result {
    if (z < 1 || z > 255) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (n_lower < 1 || n_lower > 0xFFFFFFFF) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (n_upper < 1 || n_upper > 0xFFFFFFFF) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (n_upper <= n_lower) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var e, ok = _km_bohr_transition_fast(z, n_lower, n_upper);
    if (ok == 1) { return Ok(e); }
    var a: f64 = _km_bohr_transition_acc(z, n_lower, n_upper);
    return Ok(a);
}

# The Rydberg operands as double-doubles, for 1 ≤ n₁ < n₂ < 2³² and the constant r, into `out` (four f64, 32 bytes:
# N_h, N_l, M_h, M_l) with N = r·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²) and M = (n₁·n₂)². For n₂ < 2¹³ both are exact: K = Z²·(n₂ − n₁)(n₂ + n₁)
# < 2⁴² and M < 2⁵² are integers, exact doubles, and N = r·K an exact TwoProduct (M_l = 0). Above, each is within
# 2^-102.99: r·Z² (_km_rz2), n₂² − n₁² = (n₂ − n₁)(n₂ + n₁) and n₁·n₂ exact (TwoProducts of exact doubles), then one
# _km_dd_mul each.
fn _km_rydberg_dd(r: f64, z, n_lower, n_upper, out) {
    if (n_upper < 0x2000) {
        var kf: f64 = f64_from(z * z * (n_upper - n_lower) * (n_upper + n_lower));
        var w = n_lower * n_upper;
        var mf: f64 = f64_from(w * w);
        var kh, kl = _km_two_prod(r, kf);
        var zero: f64 = 0.0;
        store64(out, kh);
        store64(out + 8, kl);
        store64(out + 16, mf);
        store64(out + 24, zero);
        return 0;
    }
    var ph, pl = _km_rz2(r, z);
    var df: f64 = f64_from(n_upper - n_lower);
    var sf: f64 = f64_from(n_upper + n_lower);
    var dh, dl = _km_two_prod(df, sf);
    var lf: f64 = f64_from(n_lower);
    var uf: f64 = f64_from(n_upper);
    var wh, wl = _km_two_prod(lf, uf);
    var nh, nl = _km_dd_mul(ph, pl, dh, dl);
    var mh, ml = _km_dd_mul(wh, wl, wh, wl);
    store64(out, nh);
    store64(out + 8, nl);
    store64(out + 16, mh);
    store64(out + 24, ml);
    return 0;
}

# The exact Rydberg integers for 1 ≤ n₁ < n₂ < 2³²: p = M·Z²·(n₂ − n₁)·(n₂ + n₁) (below 2^134) and q = (n₁·n₂)² (below
# 2^128), with the constant's significand M (r = M·2^E), into the 64-byte buffers p and q; returns E.
#must_use
fn _km_rydberg_ints(r: f64, z, n_lower, n_upper, p, q): i64 {
    var rm, re = _km_f64_int(_km_bits(r));
    var a[64];
    var b[64];
    var w[64];
    _km_bn_set(&a, rm);
    _km_bn_mul_small(&a, z * z);
    _km_bn_set(&b, n_upper - n_lower);
    _km_bn_set(&w, n_upper + n_lower);
    var d[64];
    _km_bn_mul(&d, &b, &w);
    _km_bn_mul(p, &a, &d);
    _km_bn_set(&w, n_lower);
    _km_bn_mul_small(&w, n_upper);
    _km_bn_mul(q, &w, &w);
    return re;
}

# R·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²)/(n₁²·n₂²) by the fast path: (RN, 1), or (0, 0) when the rounding test fails.
#must_use
fn _km_bohr_transition_fast(z, n_lower, n_upper): (f64, i64) {
    var r: f64 = KM_RYDBERG_EV;
    var o[32];
    _km_rydberg_dd(r, z, n_lower, n_upper, &o);
    var nh: f64 = load64(&o);
    var nl: f64 = load64(&o + 8);
    var mh: f64 = load64(&o + 16);
    var ml: f64 = load64(&o + 24);
    var y, ok = _km_dd_div_cr(nh, nl, mh, ml);
    return (y, ok);
}

# RN(R·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²)/(n₁²·n₂²)) by the exact path alone.
#must_use
fn _km_bohr_transition_acc(z, n_lower, n_upper): f64 {
    var p[64];
    var q[64];
    var r: f64 = KM_RYDBERG_EV;
    var re = _km_rydberg_ints(r, z, n_lower, n_upper, &p, &q);
    var v: f64 = _km_cr_ratio(&p, &q, re);
    return v;
}

# The vacuum wavelength in m of a hydrogen-like transition by the Rydberg formula 1/λ = R∞·Z²·(1/n₁² − 1/n₂²), with
# RYDBERG (R∞: an infinitely heavy nucleus; for ¹H about 0.05 % short, spectral_series_wavelength applies R_H). ADR 0002
# #21: the correctly rounded n₁²·n₂²/(R∞·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²)) (rounded once). Ok(λ), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) as
# _km_rydberg_formula. Mirrors `spectroscopy::rydberg_wavelength`, with ADR 0002 #21.
#must_use
fn rydberg_wavelength(z, n_lower, n_upper): Result {
    return _km_rydberg_formula(KM_RYDBERG, z, n_lower, n_upper);
}

# Rust's private rydberg_formula with the Rydberg constant `rydberg` (m⁻¹), under ADR 0002 #21: Ok(λ), the correctly
# rounded n₁²·n₂²/(rydberg·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²)), where Rust computes 1/(((R·Z)·Z)·(1/(n₁·n₁) − 1/(n₂·n₂))).
# Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if Z is 0 (or outside u8), then if either n is 0 (or outside u32), then if
# n_upper ≤ n_lower.
#must_use
fn _km_rydberg_formula(rydberg: f64, z, n_lower, n_upper): Result {
    if (z < 1 || z > 255) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (n_lower < 1 || n_lower > 0xFFFFFFFF || n_upper < 1 || n_upper > 0xFFFFFFFF) {
        return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT);
    }
    if (n_upper <= n_lower) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var lam, ok = _km_rydberg_fast(rydberg, z, n_lower, n_upper);
    if (ok == 1) { return Ok(lam); }
    var a: f64 = _km_rydberg_acc(rydberg, z, n_lower, n_upper);
    return Ok(a);
}

# n₁²·n₂²/(r·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²)) by the fast path: (RN, 1), or (0, 0) when the rounding test fails.
#must_use
fn _km_rydberg_fast(r: f64, z, n_lower, n_upper): (f64, i64) {
    var o[32];
    _km_rydberg_dd(r, z, n_lower, n_upper, &o);
    var nh: f64 = load64(&o);
    var nl: f64 = load64(&o + 8);
    var mh: f64 = load64(&o + 16);
    var ml: f64 = load64(&o + 24);
    var y, ok = _km_dd_div_cr(mh, ml, nh, nl);
    return (y, ok);
}

# RN(n₁²·n₂²/(r·Z²·(n₂² − n₁²))) by the exact path alone.
#must_use
fn _km_rydberg_acc(r: f64, z, n_lower, n_upper): f64 {
    var p[64];
    var q[64];
    var re = _km_rydberg_ints(r, z, n_lower, n_upper, &p, &q);
    var v: f64 = _km_cr_ratio(&q, &p, 0 - re);
    return v;
}

# The lower quantum number of series s (a KmSpectralSeries value): 1 (Lyman) … 5 (Pfund); 0 for a word that is no
# KmSpectralSeries value, which no series has. Mirrors `SpectralSeries::n_lower`.
#must_use
fn spectral_series_n_lower(s): i64 {
    if (_km_is_enum_value(s, KM_SPECTRAL_SERIES_COUNT) != 1) { return 0; }
    return KM_SPECTRAL_SERIES_N_LOWER[s];
}

# The vacuum wavelength in m of line `line` (1 = α, 2 = β, …; Rust's u32) of the ¹H series s (a KmSpectralSeries
# value), n_upper = n_lower + line, by the Rydberg formula with the reduced-mass-corrected R_H = R∞/(1 + mₑ/mₚ)
# (_KM_RYDBERG_HYDROGEN, Rust's const evaluation; Bohr model, no fine structure): Lyα 121.568 nm, Hα 656.470 nm.
# Ok(λ), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if s is no KmSpectralSeries value, then if `line` is 0 (or outside u32), then
# if n_lower + line overflows u32 (Rust's checked_add). Mirrors `SpectralSeries::wavelength`.
#must_use
fn spectral_series_wavelength(s, line): Result {
    if (_km_is_enum_value(s, KM_SPECTRAL_SERIES_COUNT) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (line < 1 || line > 0xFFFFFFFF) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var nl = KM_SPECTRAL_SERIES_N_LOWER[s];
    var nu = nl + line;
    if (nu > 0xFFFFFFFF) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    return _km_rydberg_formula(_KM_RYDBERG_HYDROGEN, 1, nl, nu);
}

# ── wavenumber conversions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The wavenumber ν̃ = 1/(λ in cm) in cm⁻¹ from the wavelength λ (m). ADR 0002 #19: the correctly rounded 1/(100·λ)
# (_km_cr_hundredth_div), where Rust's `0.01 / λ` rounds 1/100 first. Ok(ν̃), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if λ is not
# positive and finite. Mirrors `spectroscopy::wavenumber_from_wavelength`, with ADR 0002 #19.
#must_use
fn wavenumber_from_wavelength(wavelength_m: f64): Result {
    if (_km_is_positive(wavelength_m) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var w: f64 = _km_cr_hundredth_div(wavelength_m);
    return Ok(w);
}

# The wavelength λ = 1/ν̃ (in cm) in m from the wavenumber ν̃ (cm⁻¹). ADR 0002 #19: the correctly rounded 1/(100·ν̃)
# (_km_cr_hundredth_div), where Rust's `0.01 / ν̃` rounds 1/100 first. Ok(λ), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if ν̃ is not
# positive and finite. Mirrors `spectroscopy::wavelength_from_wavenumber`, with ADR 0002 #19.
#must_use
fn wavelength_from_wavenumber(wavenumber_cm_inv: f64): Result {
    if (_km_is_positive(wavenumber_cm_inv) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var l: f64 = _km_cr_hundredth_div(wavenumber_cm_inv);
    return Ok(l);
}

# ── harmonic oscillator ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The reduced mass of a diatomic μ = m₁·m₂/(m₁ + m₂), in the masses' unit. ADR 0002 #22 (M4e-3, the user's decision of
# 2026-11-10): the correctly rounded m₁·m₂/(m₁ + m₂) (rounded once, ties to even, subnormal results on the 2⁻¹⁰⁷⁴ grid)
# for every pair of positive finite doubles, so m/2 exactly for a homonuclear diatomic, where Rust's (m₁·m₂)/(m₁ + m₂)
# rounds three times (one ulp from m/2 on 8.6 % of masses) and overflows or underflows at the range ends (NaN for
# (DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX), 0 or ∞ where μ is representable). No intermediate leaves the range: with m₁ ≥ m₂, m = s·2^e
# (s in [1, 2)) and g = e₁ − e₂, μ = 2^e₂·s₁s₂/(s₁ + s₂·2⁻ᵍ); for g ≥ 55 it is the smaller mass (below). Ok(μ), or
# Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if either mass is not positive and finite. Mirrors `spectroscopy::reduced_mass`, with ADR
# 0002 #22.
#must_use
fn reduced_mass(m1: f64, m2: f64): Result {
    if (_km_is_positive(m1) != 1 || _km_is_positive(m2) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var mu, ok = _km_reduced_mass_fast(m1, m2);
    if (ok == 1) { return Ok(mu); }
    var a: f64 = _km_reduced_mass_acc(m1, m2);
    return Ok(a);
}

# The reduced mass of two positive finite doubles by the fast path: (RN(μ), 1), or (0, 0) when the rounding test fails
# or μ may be subnormal (e₂ < −1021). With m₁ ≥ m₂ (as bit patterns), m₁ = s₁·2^e₁, m₂ = s₂·2^e₂ and g = e₁ − e₂ ≥ 0
# (from the exponent fields: for a subnormal m₂ a lower bound of the gap, which only makes the first case rarer):
# - g ≥ 55: RN(μ) = m₂. x = m₂/m₁ < 2^(1 − g) ≤ 2⁻⁵⁴, and m₂ − μ = m₂·x/(1 + x) < 2⁻⁵⁴·m₂, below half the gap from m₂
#   to the double under it (at least 2⁻⁵³·m₂ for a normal m₂, the gap below a power of two included; 2⁻¹⁰⁷⁴ for a
#   subnormal one, against m₂·x < 2⁻¹⁰⁷⁶), and μ < m₂: strictly inside m₂'s rounding interval, so never a tie.
# - otherwise F = s₁s₂/(s₁ + s₂·2⁻ᵍ) in [½, 2): the numerator an exact TwoProduct in [1, 4), s₂·2⁻ᵍ exact (at least
#   2⁻⁵⁴) and the denominator an exact FastTwoSum in [1, 4) (s₁ ≥ s₂·2⁻ᵍ), so _km_dd_div_cr's operands carry no error;
#   RN(μ) = RN(F)·2^e₂, exact for e₂ ≥ −1021 (μ ≥ 2⁻¹⁰²², and RN(μ) ≤ m₂ ≤ DBL_MAX).
#must_use
fn _km_reduced_mass_fast(m1: f64, m2: f64): (f64, i64) {
    var b1 = load64(&m1);
    var b2 = load64(&m2);
    if (b1 < b2) {
        var sw = b1;
        b1 = b2;
        b2 = sw;
    }
    var g = (b1 >> 52) - (b2 >> 52);                # e₁ − e₂ (a lower bound when m₂ is subnormal)
    if (g >= 55) {
        var small: f64 = b2;
        return (small, 1);
    }
    if (b2 < 0x0020000000000000) { return (0.0, 0); }  # e₂ < −1021: μ may be subnormal
    var sb1 = (b1 & 0x000FFFFFFFFFFFFF) | 0x3FF0000000000000;
    var sb2 = (b2 & 0x000FFFFFFFFFFFFF) | 0x3FF0000000000000;
    var s1: f64 = sb1;
    var s2: f64 = sb2;
    var scb = (1023 - g) << 52;
    var sc: f64 = scb;
    var t: f64 = s2 * sc;                           # s₂·2⁻ᵍ, exact
    var ph, pl = _km_two_prod(s1, s2);
    var dh: f64 = s1 + t;                           # FastTwoSum: s₁ ≥ t (s₁ ≥ s₂ when g = 0, t < 1 otherwise)
    var dl: f64 = t - (dh - s1);
    var f, ok = _km_dd_div_cr(ph, pl, dh, dl);
    if (ok != 1) { return (0.0, 0); }
    var eb = b2 & 0x7FF0000000000000;               # 2^e₂
    var s3: f64 = eb;
    var mu: f64 = f * s3;
    return (mu, 1);
}

# RN(m₁·m₂/(m₁ + m₂)) by the exact path alone, every pair of positive finite doubles: with m₁ ≥ m₂, m = M·2^E
# (2⁵² ≤ M < 2⁵³, a subnormal's E below −1074) and G = E₁ − E₂ (the g above), μ = M₁M₂·2^E₁/(M₁·2^G + M₂), rounded once
# by _km_cr_ratio (the numerator below 2¹⁰⁶, the denominator below 2¹⁰⁸); m₂ for G ≥ 55 (the fast path's proof).
#must_use
fn _km_reduced_mass_acc(m1: f64, m2: f64): f64 {
    var b1 = _km_bits(m1);
    var b2 = _km_bits(m2);
    if (b1 < b2) {
        var sw = b1;
        b1 = b2;
        b2 = sw;
    }
    var mm1, ee1 = _km_f64_int(b1);
    var mm2, ee2 = _km_f64_int(b2);
    var gg = ee1 - ee2;
    if (gg >= 55) {
        var small: f64 = b2;
        return small;
    }
    var a[64];
    var b[64];
    var p[64];
    var q[64];
    _km_bn_set(&a, mm1);
    _km_bn_set(&b, mm2);
    _km_bn_mul(&p, &a, &b);
    _km_bn_shl(&a, gg);
    _km_bn_add(&q, &a, &b);
    var v: f64 = _km_cr_ratio(&p, &q, ee1);
    return v;
}

# The quantum harmonic oscillator energy E_n = (n + ½)·ħω in J, from the vibrational quantum number n (Rust's u32) and
# the angular frequency ω (rad/s), as Rust's ((n + 0.5)·ħ)·ω with ħ = h/(2π) (2π = 2·std::f64::consts::PI, exact).
# Inputs are not validated; NaN for an n outside 0 ..= u32::MAX (there is no error channel). Mirrors
# `spectroscopy::harmonic_oscillator_energy`.
#must_use
fn harmonic_oscillator_energy(n, angular_frequency: f64): f64 {
    if (_km_is_u32(n) != 1) { return _km_nan(); }
    var pi: f64 = KM_PI;                    # std::f64::consts::PI
    var tp: f64 = 2.0 * pi;
    var hbar: f64 = KM_PLANCK / tp;
    var nf: f64 = f64_from(n);
    var nh: f64 = nf + 0.5;
    var e: f64 = (nh * hbar) * angular_frequency;
    return e;
}

# The angular vibrational frequency ω = √(k/μ) in rad/s from the force constant k (N/m) and the reduced mass μ (kg).
# Ok(ω), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if k, then μ, is not positive and finite. ADR 0002 #30: the correctly rounded
# √(k/μ) of the exact quotient (src/mathx.cyr's _km_cr_sqrt_div), where Rust's (k/μ).sqrt() rounds twice and k/μ can
# over- or underflow (ω is finite and nonzero for every positive finite k and μ but a subnormal μ's largest quotients,
# past DBL_MAX², where it is +∞). Mirrors `spectroscopy::vibrational_frequency`, with ADR 0002 #30.
#must_use
fn vibrational_frequency(force_constant: f64, reduced_mass_kg: f64): Result {
    if (_km_is_positive(force_constant) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (_km_is_positive(reduced_mass_kg) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var w: f64 = _km_cr_sqrt_div(force_constant, reduced_mass_kg);
    return Ok(w);
}

# ── rotational spectroscopy ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The rigid-rotor energy E_J = h·B·J(J + 1) in J, from the rotational quantum number J (Rust's u32) and the rotational
# constant B (Hz). Inputs are not validated; NaN for a J outside 0 ..= u32::MAX. ADR 0002 #30: the correctly rounded
# h·B·J·(J + 1) for a finite B (J = 0 gives B's signed zero, J and J + 1 are exact), where Rust's ((h·B)·J)·(J + 1)
# rounds three times and h·B underflows for |B| below 3.4·10⁻²⁷⁵; a non-finite B gives Rust's expression. Mirrors
# `spectroscopy::rigid_rotor_energy`, with ADR 0002 #30.
#must_use
fn rigid_rotor_energy(j, rotational_constant_hz: f64): f64 {
    if (_km_is_u32(j) != 1) { return _km_nan(); }
    var jf: f64 = f64_from(j);
    var j1: f64 = jf + 1.0;
    var h: f64 = KM_PLANCK;
    if (_km_is_finite(rotational_constant_hz) != 1) {
        var e: f64 = ((h * rotational_constant_hz) * jf) * j1;
        return e;
    }
    var r: f64 = _km_cr_mul4(h, rotational_constant_hz, jf, j1);
    return r;
}

# The rotational constant B = h/(8π²I) in Hz from the moment of inertia I (kg·m²). Ok(B), or Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT)
# if I is not positive and finite. ADR 0002 #30: the correctly rounded h/(8·π·π·I) with π as its double (Rust's
# h/(((8·π)·π)·I) exactly), computed as (h/8)/(π·π·I) (h/8 exact), where Rust rounds three times and 8π²·I is subnormal
# for I below 2.8·10⁻³¹⁰. Mirrors `spectroscopy::rotational_constant`, with ADR 0002 #30.
#must_use
fn rotational_constant(moment_of_inertia: f64): Result {
    if (_km_is_positive(moment_of_inertia) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var pi: f64 = KM_PI;                    # std::f64::consts::PI
    var h: f64 = KM_PLANCK;
    var h8: f64 = h * 0.125;
    var b: f64 = _km_cr_div3(h8, pi, pi, moment_of_inertia);
    return Ok(b);
}

# The moment of inertia of a diatomic I = μ·r² in kg·m² from the reduced mass μ (kg) and the bond length r (m). Inputs
# are not validated. ADR 0002 #30: the correctly rounded μ·r·r for finite inputs (a zero factor gives its signed zero),
# where Rust's (μ·r)·r rounds twice; a non-finite input gives Rust's (μ·r)·r. Mirrors
# `spectroscopy::diatomic_moment_of_inertia`, with ADR 0002 #30.
#must_use
fn diatomic_moment_of_inertia(reduced_mass_kg: f64, bond_length_m: f64): f64 {
    var f, ok = _km_mul3_fast(reduced_mass_kg, bond_length_m, bond_length_m);
    if (ok == 1) { return f; }
    var r: f64 = _km_mul3_rest(reduced_mass_kg, bond_length_m, bond_length_m);
    return r;
}

# ── mass-spectrometry isotope patterns ──────────────────────────────────────────────

# The natural isotope abundances of the element with atomic number z (Rust's u8): (pointer, n) to n (isotope mass in
# u, abundance as a mole fraction) pairs, 16 bytes each (the two f64), in the baked table (src/spectroscopy_data.cyr).
# ADR 0002 #23 (M4e-4, the user's decision): the masses are AME2020's at full published precision (Wang et al., Chinese
# Physics C 45, 030003 (2021)), the abundances the IUPAC 2009 representative isotopic compositions (TICE 2009: Berglund
# and Wieser, Pure Appl. Chem. 83, 397-410 (2011)), ³⁶S included, from the cited records in scripts/oracle/isotopes.tsv;
# Rust 1.2.0's literal rounds the masses to five decimals (¹H 1.00783, not 1.007825031898) and omits ³⁶S, so its sulfur
# sums to 0.9999. Only elements with significant isotope patterns have data (H, C, N, O, S, Cl, Br: sulfur 4 isotopes,
# the others Rust's); every other z, and a word outside 0 ..= 255, gives the empty slice (0, 0). Mirrors
# `spectroscopy::isotope_abundances`, with that table.
#must_use
fn isotope_abundances(atomic_number): (i64, i64) {
    if (atomic_number < 0 || atomic_number >= _KM_ISOTOPE_ABUNDANCE_ZS) { return (0, 0); }
    var n = _KM_ISOTOPE_ABUNDANCE_COUNT[atomic_number];
    if (n == 0) { return (0, 0); }
    var p = &_KM_ISOTOPE_ABUNDANCES + 16 * _KM_ISOTOPE_ABUNDANCE_START[atomic_number];
    return (p, n);
}

# The total-order key of the f64 bits b (Rust's total_cmp compares these as i64: the magnitude bits of a negative
# pattern flipped, so −NaN < −∞ < … < −0 < +0 < … < +∞ < +NaN).
#must_use
fn _km_total_key(b): i64 {
    return b ^ ((b >>> 63) >> 1);
}

# Sorts the n (mass, intensity) records at buf by mass in IEEE total order, stably (equal keys keep their order):
# Rust's `sort_by(|a, b| a.0.total_cmp(&b.0))`, a stable sort, so the same permutation as any stable sort. Insertion
# sort: the scratch is the pattern's sorted run interleaved with the isotopes', each record at most a few places from
# its position, so it is near linear here.
fn _km_isotope_sort(buf, n) {
    var i = 1;
    while (i < n) {
        var mb = load64(buf + 16 * i);
        var ib = load64(buf + 16 * i + 8);
        var key = mb ^ ((mb >>> 63) >> 1);  # _km_total_key, inline here and below
        var j = i;
        while (j > 0) {
            var pb = load64(buf + 16 * (j - 1));
            if ((pb ^ ((pb >>> 63) >> 1)) <= key) { break; }
            store64(buf + 16 * j, pb);
            store64(buf + 16 * j + 8, load64(buf + 16 * (j - 1) + 8));
            j = j - 1;
        }
        store64(buf + 16 * j, mb);
        store64(buf + 16 * j + 8, ib);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# Rust's binning: the n sorted records at src merged into dst, a record joining the last bin when its mass is within
# ISOTOPE_BIN_WIDTH_U (0.5 u, typed inline) of the bin's (|m − m_last| < 0.5; NaN starts a bin) — the bin's mass then
# the intensity-weighted mean (m_last·i_last + m·i)/total when total > 0, its intensity the sum. Returns the bin count.
#must_use
fn _km_isotope_bin(src, n, dst): i64 {
    var width: f64 = 0.5;                   # ISOTOPE_BIN_WIDTH_U (u)
    var np = 0;
    var k = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        var m: f64 = load64(src + 16 * k);
        var i: f64 = load64(src + 16 * k + 8);
        var merged = 0;
        if (np > 0) {
            var lm: f64 = load64(dst + 16 * (np - 1));
            var li: f64 = load64(dst + 16 * (np - 1) + 8);
            var dm: f64 = m - lm;
            var adm: f64 = f64_abs(dm);
            if (adm < width) {
                var total: f64 = li + i;
                if (total > 0.0) {
                    var mean: f64 = ((lm * li) + (m * i)) / total;
                    store64(dst + 16 * (np - 1), mean);
                }
                store64(dst + 16 * (np - 1) + 8, total);
                merged = 1;
            }
        }
        if (merged == 0) {
            store64(dst + 16 * np, m);
            store64(dst + 16 * np + 8, i);
            np = np + 1;
        }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    return np;
}

# Rust's renormalisation and pruning of the n records at buf: the base intensity fold(0.0, max) (NaN ignored), every
# intensity divided by it when it is > 0, then `retain(|p| p.1 >= ISOTOPE_PRUNE_REL)` (10⁻¹², typed inline) in order.
# Returns the count kept.
#must_use
fn _km_isotope_normalize(buf, n): i64 {
    var prune: f64 = 0.000000000001;        # ISOTOPE_PRUNE_REL, 10⁻¹²
    var mx: f64 = 0.0;
    var k = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        var i: f64 = load64(buf + 16 * k + 8);
        # mx = _km_max(mx, i), inline: mx is never NaN, so a NaN i keeps mx, and otherwise the larger (i on a tie).
        if (i == i && !(mx > i)) { mx = i; }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (mx > 0.0) {
        k = 0;
        while (k < n) {
            var i2: f64 = load64(buf + 16 * k + 8);
            var r: f64 = i2 / mx;
            store64(buf + 16 * k + 8, r);
            k = k + 1;
        }
    }
    var kept = 0;
    k = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        var i3: f64 = load64(buf + 16 * k + 8);
        if (i3 >= prune) {
            store64(buf + 16 * kept, load64(buf + 16 * k));
            store64(buf + 16 * kept + 8, load64(buf + 16 * k + 8));
            kept = kept + 1;
        }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    return kept;
}

# Rust's final filter `retain(|(_, i)| *i > ISOTOPE_REPORT_REL)` (0.001, typed inline) of the n records at buf, in
# order; returns the count kept.
#must_use
fn _km_isotope_report(buf, n): i64 {
    var report: f64 = 0.001;                # ISOTOPE_REPORT_REL
    var kept = 0;
    var k = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        var i: f64 = load64(buf + 16 * k + 8);
        if (i > report) {
            store64(buf + 16 * kept, load64(buf + 16 * k));
            store64(buf + 16 * kept + 8, load64(buf + 16 * k + 8));
            kept = kept + 1;
        }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    return kept;
}

# The isotope pattern (mass-spectrum envelope) of a molecule: its n (atomic number, count) records at `atoms`, 16 bytes
# each (Z as a word, then the count as a word: Rust's `&[(u8, u32)]`), convolved atom by atom from the single peak
# (0, 1): for an element with isotope data, `count` times, each step every peak × every isotope (masses added,
# intensities multiplied), sorted by mass (stably), peaks within 0.5 u merged (intensity-weighted mean mass), the
# intensities renormalised to the base peak and those below 10⁻¹² of it pruned; an element without data shifts every
# peak by count × its standard atomic weight (not its monoisotopic mass: Rust's documented limitation). Finally peaks
# at or below 0.1 % of the base peak are dropped. The (mass in u, relative intensity) peaks, ascending in mass, the
# base peak 1.0, go to the caller's buffer `out` of `cap` 16-byte records (mass, then intensity): Ok(count).
# Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if the molecule is empty (n = 0) or (atoms, n) is no slice; per atom in order,
# Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_ELEMENT) for a Z outside 0 ..= 255, or with no data and no element (0, > 118), then
# Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) for a count outside 0 ..= u32::MAX; Err(KM_ERR_COMPUTATION_ERROR) if a working table can't
# be allocated; Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if the pattern has more than cap peaks (`out` untouched). Mirrors
# `spectroscopy::isotope_pattern`.
#must_use
fn isotope_pattern(atoms, n, out, cap): Result {
    var slots: i64[2];
    store64(&slots, 0 - 1);
    store64(&slots + 8, 0 - 1);
    var t, v = _km_isotope_pattern_on(atoms, n, out, cap, &slots);
    _km_scratch_give(load64(&slots));
    _km_scratch_give(load64(&slots + 8));
    if (is_ok(t) == 1) { return Ok(v); }
    return Err(v);
}

# isotope_pattern's body: past _KM_ISO_STACK records its two working tables move from the stack to two buffers of the
# work-table pool, whose slots it records at slots (the pattern's) and slots + 8 (the scratch's) for the caller to give
# back.
#must_use
fn _km_isotope_pattern_on(atoms, n, out, cap, slots): Result {
    if (_km_is_slice(atoms, n) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (n == 0) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var pst: f64[_KM_ISO_STACK * 2];        # _KM_ISO_STACK records each
    var sst: f64[_KM_ISO_STACK * 2];
    var pat = &pst;
    var scr = &sst;
    var heap = 0;
    var tcap = _KM_ISO_STACK;
    var zero: f64 = 0.0;
    var one: f64 = 1.0;
    store64(pat, zero);                     # the single peak (0, 1)
    store64(pat + 8, one);
    var np = 1;
    var a = 0;
    while (a < n) {
        var z = load64(atoms + 16 * a);
        var count = load64(atoms + 16 * a + 8);
        if (_km_is_u8(z) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_ELEMENT); }
        var ab, na = isotope_abundances(z);
        var h = 0;
        if (na == 0) {
            h = element_lookup_by_number(z);
            if (h == 0) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_ELEMENT); }
        }
        if (_km_is_u32(count) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
        if (na == 0) {
            # No isotope data: shift every peak by count × the standard atomic weight.
            var cf: f64 = f64_from(count);
            var am: f64 = element_atomic_mass(h);
            var shift: f64 = cf * am;
            var k = 0;
            while (k < np) {
                var m: f64 = load64(pat + 16 * k);
                var ms: f64 = m + shift;
                store64(pat + 16 * k, ms);
                k = k + 1;
            }
        } else {
            var step = 0;
            while (step < count) {
                if (np * na > tcap) {
                    var ncap = 2 * tcap;
                    if (ncap < np * na) { ncap = np * na; }
                    if (ncap > 0x0800000000000000 / 16) { return Err(KM_ERR_COMPUTATION_ERROR); }
                    var npat = 0;
                    var nscr = 0;
                    if (heap == 0) {
                        var q1, s1 = _km_scratch_take(16 * ncap);
                        store64(slots, s1);
                        var q2, s2 = _km_scratch_take(16 * ncap);
                        store64(slots + 8, s2);
                        if (q1 != 0) {
                            var kc = 0;
                            while (kc < 2 * np) {
                                store64(q1 + 8 * kc, load64(pat + 8 * kc));
                                kc = kc + 1;
                            }
                        }
                        npat = q1;
                        nscr = q2;
                        heap = 1;
                    } else {
                        npat = _km_scratch_grow(load64(slots), pat, 16 * ncap, 16 * np);
                        nscr = _km_scratch_grow(load64(slots + 8), scr, 16 * ncap, 0);
                    }
                    if (npat == 0 || nscr == 0) { return Err(KM_ERR_COMPUTATION_ERROR); }
                    pat = npat;
                    scr = nscr;
                    tcap = ncap;
                }
                # Convolve with this element's isotopes: scratch[k·na + j] = (m₁ + m₂, i₁·i₂).
                var ns = 0;
                var k1 = 0;
                while (k1 < np) {
                    var m1: f64 = load64(pat + 16 * k1);
                    var i1: f64 = load64(pat + 16 * k1 + 8);
                    var j = 0;
                    while (j < na) {
                        var m2: f64 = load64(ab + 16 * j);
                        var i2: f64 = load64(ab + 16 * j + 8);
                        var ms2: f64 = m1 + m2;
                        var is2: f64 = i1 * i2;
                        store64(scr + 16 * ns, ms2);
                        store64(scr + 16 * ns + 8, is2);
                        ns = ns + 1;
                        j = j + 1;
                    }
                    k1 = k1 + 1;
                }
                _km_isotope_sort(scr, ns);
                np = _km_isotope_bin(scr, ns, pat);
                np = _km_isotope_normalize(pat, np);
                step = step + 1;
            }
        }
        a = a + 1;
    }
    np = _km_isotope_report(pat, np);
    if (cap < 0 || out == 0 || np > cap) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var w = 0;
    while (w < 2 * np) {
        store64(out + 8 * w, load64(pat + 8 * w));
        w = w + 1;
    }
    return Ok(np);
}

# ── Fourier-transform spectroscopy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

# hisab 1.4.0's `num::fft` (src/num/fft.rs), transcribed (ADR 0003, M4e amendment): the in-place radix-2 Cooley–Tukey
# DFT of the n complex values at `data` (re, im: 16 bytes each), n a power of two (the caller's check; n ≤ 1 is
# returned unchanged, as 1.4.0's `n <= 1` exit). Its bit-reversal permutation, then per stage len = 2, 4, …, n the
# twiddle wn = (cos θ, sin θ) of θ = (−2·π)/len (π = std::f64::consts::PI; −2·π is exact, the ÷ len too) from the
# correctly rounded _km_cr_sincos, and per block w = (1, 0), each butterfly t = w·d (Complex's mul: re = w.re·d.re −
# w.im·d.im, im = w.re·d.im + w.im·d.re), u + t and u − t, then w *= wn (mul_assign, the same products): every
# operation in hisab's order, no FMA, so the result is the oracle's with correctly rounded twiddles. The recurrence is
# input-independent, the same in every block, so the port runs it once a stage, the block loop inside it (2.0.1).
#
# ADR 0002 #36 (the overflow audit's B6): it also returns 1 when its products provably stayed in binary64's normal
# range, else 0 — every component of wn, of each twiddle w a butterfly uses (checked once a stage, as it is computed)
# and of each butterfly's d is 0 or of magnitude at least
# 2⁻¹⁰⁰ (wn, w) or 2⁻⁹²¹ (d). Then every product of the transform (w·d and the recurrence's w·wn) is an exact zero (a
# zero factor) or its exact value is at least 2⁻¹⁰²¹ in magnitude, where binary64's rounding is the 53-bit one unless
# it overflows; and every sum of two doubles is exact below 2⁻¹⁰²¹ and the 53-bit rounding above unless it overflows
# (the pure-sum lemma, ADR 0002 #33). So the transform is the unbounded-exponent one (#20's semantics) unless some
# operation overflowed; fft_spectrum tests that on the retained outputs (an overflow reaches one of them as ±∞ or NaN).
# The twiddles' bound never binds in practice (their smallest nonzero component is 2⁻⁵⁶ for every n ≤ 2²², wn's at
# least 2⁻⁶⁰ for every n), so the test is the data's: a d below 2⁻⁹²¹ ≈ 5.64·10⁻²⁷⁸ takes the slow path.
#must_use
fn _km_fft(data, n): i64 {
    if (n <= 1) { return 1; }
    var j = 0;
    var i = 1;
    while (i < n) {
        var bit = n >> 1;
        while ((j & bit) != 0) {
            j = j ^ bit;
            bit = bit >> 1;
        }
        j = j ^ bit;
        if (i < j) {
            var ar = load64(data + 16 * i);
            var ai = load64(data + 16 * i + 8);
            store64(data + 16 * i, load64(data + 16 * j));
            store64(data + 16 * i + 8, load64(data + 16 * j + 8));
            store64(data + 16 * j, ar);
            store64(data + 16 * j + 8, ai);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    var ok = 1;
    var lw: f64 = 0x39B0000000000000;       # 2⁻¹⁰⁰: the twiddles' bound
    var pi: f64 = KM_PI;                    # std::f64::consts::PI
    var m2pi: f64 = -2.0 * pi;
    var len = 2;
    while (len <= n) {
        var half = len >> 1;
        var lf: f64 = f64_from(len);
        var angle: f64 = m2pi / lf;
        var wn_im, wn_re = _km_cr_sincos(angle);
        var awr: f64 = f64_abs(wn_re);
        var awi: f64 = f64_abs(wn_im);
        if ((awr < lw && awr != 0.0) || (awi < lw && awi != 0.0)) { ok = 0; }
        # The twiddle loop outside the block loop (2.0.1): hisab's recurrence restarts at w = (1, 0) in every block and
        # is input-independent, so twiddle k is the same in each, computed and tested once; the butterflies of one
        # stage touch disjoint pairs, so their order does not matter. The data test on the bits: |d| is 0 or at least
        # 2⁻⁹²¹ exactly when (bits & 0x7FF…F) is 0 or at least 0x066…0 (a NaN's magnitude bits are above it, as
        # NaN < 2⁻⁹²¹ is false).
        var w_re: f64 = 1.0;
        var w_im: f64 = 0.0;
        var k = 0;
        while (k < half) {
            var awr2: f64 = f64_abs(w_re);
            var awi2: f64 = f64_abs(w_im);
            if ((awr2 < lw && awr2 != 0.0) || (awi2 < lw && awi2 != 0.0)) { ok = 0; }
            var start = 0;
            while (start < n) {
                var pu = data + 16 * (start + k);
                var pt = data + 16 * (start + k + half);
                var u_re: f64 = load64(pu);
                var u_im: f64 = load64(pu + 8);
                var d_re: f64 = load64(pt);
                var d_im: f64 = load64(pt + 8);
                var bdr = load64(pt) & 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
                var bdi = load64(pt + 8) & 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
                if (bdr < 0x0660000000000000) { if (bdr != 0) { ok = 0; } }
                if (bdi < 0x0660000000000000) { if (bdi != 0) { ok = 0; } }
                var t_re: f64 = (w_re * d_re) - (w_im * d_im);
                var t_im: f64 = (w_re * d_im) + (w_im * d_re);
                var s_re: f64 = u_re + t_re;
                var s_im: f64 = u_im + t_im;
                var v_re: f64 = u_re - t_re;
                var v_im: f64 = u_im - t_im;
                store64(pu, s_re);
                store64(pu + 8, s_im);
                store64(pt, v_re);
                store64(pt + 8, v_im);
                start = start + len;
            }
            var nw_re: f64 = (w_re * wn_re) - (w_im * wn_im);
            var nw_im: f64 = (w_re * wn_im) + (w_im * wn_re);
            w_re = nw_re;
            w_im = nw_im;
            k = k + 1;
        }
        len = len << 1;
    }
    return ok;
}

# ADR 0002 #36: _km_fft's transform with an unbounded exponent (#20's semantics): every + − × of the bit-reversed
# butterflies and of the twiddle recurrence on src/mathx.cyr's _km_ux_* (53 bits, nothing over- or underflows; the same
# operations in the same order, signed zeros as IEEE's), from the n finite doubles at `sig` as (x, +0.0), into the 32·n
# bytes at `w`: per value re = (s, e), im = (s, e), two words each.
fn _km_fft_ux(sig, n, w) {
    var zero: f64 = 0.0;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        var x: f64 = load64(sig + 8 * i);
        var xs, xe = _km_ux_from(x);
        store64(w + 32 * i, xs);
        store64(w + 32 * i + 8, xe);
        store64(w + 32 * i + 16, zero);
        store64(w + 32 * i + 24, 0);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (n <= 1) { return 0; }
    var j = 0;
    i = 1;
    while (i < n) {
        var bit = n >> 1;
        while ((j & bit) != 0) {
            j = j ^ bit;
            bit = bit >> 1;
        }
        j = j ^ bit;
        if (i < j) {
            var q = 0;
            while (q < 4) {
                var a = load64(w + 32 * i + 8 * q);
                store64(w + 32 * i + 8 * q, load64(w + 32 * j + 8 * q));
                store64(w + 32 * j + 8 * q, a);
                q = q + 1;
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    var pi: f64 = KM_PI;
    var m2pi: f64 = -2.0 * pi;
    var len = 2;
    while (len <= n) {
        var half = len >> 1;
        var lf: f64 = f64_from(len);
        var angle: f64 = m2pi / lf;
        var wn_im, wn_re = _km_cr_sincos(angle);
        var nrs, nre = _km_ux_from(wn_re);
        var nis, nie = _km_ux_from(wn_im);
        # The twiddle loop outside the block loop, as in _km_fft (2.0.1): each twiddle's recurrence step once a stage.
        var wrs: f64 = 1.0;
        var wre = 0;
        var wis: f64 = 0.0;
        var wie = 0;
        var k = 0;
        while (k < half) {
            var start = 0;
            while (start < n) {
                var pu = w + 32 * (start + k);
                var pt = w + 32 * (start + k + half);
                var urs: f64 = load64(pu);
                var ure = load64(pu + 8);
                var uis: f64 = load64(pu + 16);
                var uie = load64(pu + 24);
                var drs: f64 = load64(pt);
                var dre = load64(pt + 8);
                var dis: f64 = load64(pt + 16);
                var die = load64(pt + 24);
                var p1s, p1e = _km_ux_mul(wrs, wre, drs, dre);
                var p2s, p2e = _km_ux_mul(wis, wie, dis, die);
                var trs, tre = _km_ux_sub(p1s, p1e, p2s, p2e);
                var p3s, p3e = _km_ux_mul(wrs, wre, dis, die);
                var p4s, p4e = _km_ux_mul(wis, wie, drs, dre);
                var tis, tie = _km_ux_add(p3s, p3e, p4s, p4e);
                var ars, are = _km_ux_add(urs, ure, trs, tre);
                var ais, aie = _km_ux_add(uis, uie, tis, tie);
                var brs, bre = _km_ux_sub(urs, ure, trs, tre);
                var bis, bie = _km_ux_sub(uis, uie, tis, tie);
                store64(pu, ars);
                store64(pu + 8, are);
                store64(pu + 16, ais);
                store64(pu + 24, aie);
                store64(pt, brs);
                store64(pt + 8, bre);
                store64(pt + 16, bis);
                store64(pt + 24, bie);
                start = start + len;
            }
            var q1s, q1e = _km_ux_mul(wrs, wre, nrs, nre);
            var q2s, q2e = _km_ux_mul(wis, wie, nis, nie);
            var xrs, xre = _km_ux_sub(q1s, q1e, q2s, q2e);
            var q3s, q3e = _km_ux_mul(wrs, wre, nis, nie);
            var q4s, q4e = _km_ux_mul(wis, wie, nrs, nre);
            var xis, xie = _km_ux_add(q3s, q3e, q4s, q4e);
            wrs = xrs;
            wre = xre;
            wis = xis;
            wie = xie;
            k = k + 1;
        }
        len = len << 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# ADR 0002 #36: |X| = √(re·re + im·im) of an unbounded-exponent value, the two squares and their sum to 53 bits, the √
# rounded once into binary64 (its subnormal grid, +∞ past DBL_MAX: a sum past DBL_MAX can have a finite √, and a √
# past it an in-range square sum never does).
#must_use
fn _km_fft_mag_ux(rs: f64, re, is: f64, ie): f64 {
    var as_, ae = _km_ux_mul(rs, re, rs, re);
    var bs, be = _km_ux_mul(is, ie, is, ie);
    var ss, se = _km_ux_add(as_, ae, bs, be);
    var r: f64 = _km_ux_sqrt_f64(ss, se);
    return r;
}

# ADR 0002 #36: the magnitude spectrum by the unbounded-exponent transform alone (the slow path, which fft_spectrum
# takes for a finite signal whose IEEE transform left the normal range; forced by the tests and cr_verify.py): the n
# finite values at `signal`, n a power of two, transformed by _km_fft_ux into 32·n bytes of the work-table pool
# (src/validate.cyr), then the first n/2 + 1 magnitudes by _km_fft_mag_ux to `out` (n/2 + 1 f64; `out` may overlap
# `signal`). 1, or 0 if the work table can't be allocated.
#must_use
fn _km_fft_spectrum_ux(signal, n, out): i64 {
    if (n > 0x0040000000000000) { return 0; }
    var w, ws = _km_scratch_take(32 * n);
    if (w == 0) { return 0; }
    _km_fft_ux(signal, n, w);
    var m = n / 2 + 1;
    var k = 0;
    while (k < m) {
        var rs: f64 = load64(w + 32 * k);
        var is: f64 = load64(w + 32 * k + 16);
        var mag: f64 = _km_fft_mag_ux(rs, load64(w + 32 * k + 8), is, load64(w + 32 * k + 24));
        store64(out + 8 * k, mag);
        k = k + 1;
    }
    _km_scratch_give(ws);
    return 1;
}

# ADR 0002 #36: the fast path, Rust's IEEE transform (_km_fft) of the n values at `signal` in the 16·n bytes at `data`
# (not overlapping `signal`), the n/2 + 1 magnitudes left in data's first words (word k is bin k/2's, already read).
# Where _km_fft's test passed and every retained re and im is finite, the transform is the unbounded-exponent one, and
# each bin's magnitude is Rust's √(re·re + im·im) where s = re·re + im·im lies in [2⁻⁹⁰⁰, DBL_MAX] or comes from
# re = im = 0 (#20's square-sum argument, distance_3d's in two dimensions: no square overflowed, and one below 2⁻¹⁰²¹ in
# either evaluation is added to one of at least 2⁻⁹⁰¹, under a quarter of its ulp, so s is the unbounded sum, a normal
# double or +0 whose √ is correctly rounded), else _km_fft_mag_ux of the (exact) re and im: 2 when every bin took
# Rust's, 1 when some took the unbounded magnitude. Otherwise Rust's IEEE magnitudes, and 0. One pass: a bin's
# unbounded magnitude is computed before a later bin may show the transform invalid (a non-finite re or im), which then
# sends a finite signal to the slow path, which writes every bin; and a NaN or ±∞ sample makes every output non-finite
# (it reaches both outputs of each butterfly it enters, so all n after log₂ n stages), so it takes no such magnitude.
#must_use
fn _km_fft_spectrum_fast(signal, n, data): i64 {
    var zero: f64 = 0.0;
    var i = n - 1;
    while (i >= 0) {
        var x = load64(signal + 8 * i);
        store64(data + 16 * i + 8, zero);
        store64(data + 16 * i, x);
        i = i - 1;
    }
    var ok = _km_fft(data, n);
    var m = n / 2 + 1;
    var lo: f64 = 0x07B0000000000000;       # 2⁻⁹⁰⁰
    var hi: f64 = 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF;       # DBL_MAX
    var slow = 0;
    var k = 0;
    while (k < m) {
        var re: f64 = load64(data + 16 * k);
        var im: f64 = load64(data + 16 * k + 8);
        var s: f64 = (re * re) + (im * im);
        var mag: f64 = f64_sqrt(s);
        if (s < lo || s > hi || s != s) {
            var dr: f64 = re - re;
            var di: f64 = im - im;
            if (dr != 0.0 || di != 0.0) {
                ok = 0;                     # ±∞ or NaN (x − x is NaN): an overflow, or such a sample
            } elif (ok == 1 && (re != 0.0 || im != 0.0)) {
                var rs, rx = _km_ux_from(re);
                var is, ix = _km_ux_from(im);
                mag = _km_fft_mag_ux(rs, rx, is, ix);
                slow = 1;
            }
        }
        store64(data + 8 * k, mag);
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (ok != 1) { return 0; }
    if (slow == 1) { return 1; }
    return 2;
}

# The magnitude spectrum of a real signal (an interferogram → its spectrum): the n values at `signal`, n a non-zero
# power of two, as complex numbers (x, +0.0) (Complex::from_real), transformed by the FFT (_km_fft, hisab 1.4.0's),
# then the first n/2 + 1 magnitudes √(re·re + im·im) (Complex::abs: DC up to the Nyquist bin) to the caller's buffer
# `out` of `cap` f64: Ok(n/2 + 1). Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if n is not a non-zero power of two (Rust's check), then if
# `signal` is null, then if out holds fewer than n/2 + 1 values; Err(KM_ERR_COMPUTATION_ERROR) if a work table can't be
# allocated (Rust's FFT error maps there too, unreachable).
#
# ADR 0002 #36 (the overflow audit's B6; the user's decision "FFT: both" — the magnitudes and the butterflies, hisab
# 1.4.0's transform without the range limit"): #20's semantics over the whole transform — every butterfly and twiddle
# operation in hisab's order with an unbounded exponent, and each magnitude's √ the final operation, rounded once into
# binary64 — so a signal near DBL_MAX or subnormal has its spectrum where Rust's over- or underflows
# (fft_spectrum([1.75·10³⁰⁴]) is [1.75·10³⁰⁴], Rust's [∞]; [−6.96·10⁻³¹⁶] is [6.96·10⁻³¹⁶], Rust's [0]; [3.3·10³⁰²,
# 3·10³⁰⁴] is [3.03·10³⁰⁴, 2.96·10³⁰⁴], Rust's [∞, ∞]). Rust's IEEE transform and magnitudes run first
# (_km_fft_spectrum_fast); where its per-operation test proves the transform the unbounded one, it is the result (Rust's
# bits), with Rust's magnitude of each bin whose square sum is in range and the unbounded one of any other.
# Otherwise a signal with a NaN or ±∞ keeps Rust's IEEE spectrum (B1's rule for an unvalidated input), and a finite one
# takes the unbounded transform (_km_fft_spectrum_ux, in 32·n bytes of src/validate.cyr's pool of reusable work
# tables). The IEEE transform runs in `out` itself when cap ≥ 2n and `signal` lies outside out's first 2n values
# (nothing is allocated then); otherwise in 16·n bytes of the pool, so the signal survives for the slow path (2.0.1:
# 2.0.0 took both tables from the bump allocator, which never frees, so each such call grew the heap). Mirrors
# `spectroscopy::fft_spectrum`.
#must_use
fn fft_spectrum(signal, n, out, cap): Result {
    if (n <= 0 || (n & (n - 1)) != 0) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (signal == 0) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var m = n / 2 + 1;
    if (out == 0 || cap < m) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    if (n > 0x0040000000000000) { return Err(KM_ERR_COMPUTATION_ERROR); }
    var data = out;
    var overlap = 0;
    if (signal < out + 16 * n && out < signal + 8 * n) { overlap = 1; }
    var ds = 0 - 1;
    if (cap < 2 * n || overlap == 1) {
        var q, qs = _km_scratch_take(16 * n);
        if (q == 0) { return Err(KM_ERR_COMPUTATION_ERROR); }
        data = q;
        ds = qs;
    }
    var keep = _km_fft_spectrum_fast(signal, n, data);
    if (keep == 2) { keep = 1; }
    var k = 0;
    while (keep != 1 && k < n) {
        var x: f64 = load64(signal + 8 * k);
        if (_km_is_finite(x) != 1) { keep = 1; }            # Rust's IEEE spectrum (B1's rule)
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (keep == 1) {
        if (data != out) {
            k = 0;
            while (k < m) {
                store64(out + 8 * k, load64(data + 8 * k));
                k = k + 1;
            }
        }
        _km_scratch_give(ds);
        return Ok(m);
    }
    _km_scratch_give(ds);
    if (_km_fft_spectrum_ux(signal, n, out) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_COMPUTATION_ERROR); }
    return Ok(m);
}

# ── peak detection ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# 1 if w > a·(1 − t) + b·t exactly, for finite doubles w, a, b and t (the exact rationals; 1 − t not rounded): ADR 0002
# #37's exact predicate. w − a·(1 − t) − b·t = w − a + t·a − t·b, a sum of six terms at any exponents: w and −a as
# (significand, exponent) pairs (_km_ux_from), and t·a, −t·b as exact TwoProducts of their significands (in [1, 4), the
# low part 0 or at least 2⁻¹⁰⁴) at the exponent sums; its sign by src/mathx.cyr's _km_sign_scaled. A zero factor drops
# its terms (t = 0: w > a).
#must_use
fn _km_peak_above_exact(w: f64, a: f64, b: f64, t: f64): i64 {
    var cs: f64[6];
    var ks: i64[6];
    var n = 0;
    var ws, we = _km_ux_from(w);
    store64(&cs, ws);
    store64(&ks, we);
    n = 1;
    if (a != 0.0) {
        var as_, ae = _km_ux_from(a);
        var na: f64 = -as_;
        store64(&cs + 8 * n, na);
        store64(&ks + 8 * n, ae);
        n = n + 1;
        if (t != 0.0) {
            var ts, te = _km_ux_from(t);
            var ph, pl = _km_two_prod(ts, as_);
            store64(&cs + 8 * n, ph);
            store64(&ks + 8 * n, te + ae);
            store64(&cs + 8 * n + 8, pl);
            store64(&ks + 8 * n + 8, te + ae);
            n = n + 2;
        }
    }
    if (b != 0.0 && t != 0.0) {
        var bs, be = _km_ux_from(b);
        var ts2, te2 = _km_ux_from(t);
        var qh, ql = _km_two_prod(ts2, bs);
        var nqh: f64 = -qh;
        var nql: f64 = -ql;
        store64(&cs + 8 * n, nqh);
        store64(&ks + 8 * n, te2 + be);
        store64(&cs + 8 * n + 8, nql);
        store64(&ks + 8 * n + 8, te2 + be);
        n = n + 2;
    }
    var sg = _km_sign_scaled(&cs, &ks, n);
    if (sg > 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# find_peaks' scan (the public function's body after its checks): the indices of the strict local maxima of the n ≥ 3
# values at `data` above the threshold of t, min lo and max hi of the finite values (lo ≤ hi), to `out` (room for `room`
# indices; a pass that only counts when that may be too few). (count or −1 when more peaks are found than fit, the
# number of candidates decided by the exact predicate); with `force` every candidate goes to it (the tests and
# cr_verify.py check the fast test against it). ADR 0002 #37 (the overflow audit's B6, the user's decision "find_peaks'
# threshold evaluated exactly"): a candidate w (w > its two neighbours, IEEE) is a peak when w > lo + t·(hi − lo)
# exactly. A non-finite w can only be +∞ there (NaN and −∞ fail the neighbour test), above every finite threshold. For
# a finite w the fast test decides with Rust's T = RN(RN(lo·RN(1 − t)) + RN(hi·t)) where it is finite: |T − θ| ≤
# 2.5u(1 + u)(|lo| + |hi|) + (1 + u)·2⁻¹⁰⁷⁴ (u = 2⁻⁵³: 1 − t rounds by at most u/2 for t < ½ and is exact above, the
# two products by u relative plus 2⁻¹⁰⁷⁵ each, the sum by u), below B = RN(RN(|lo|·2⁻⁵¹) + RN(|hi|·2⁻⁵¹)) + 2⁻¹⁰⁷²
# (at least (1 − u)²·(4u(|lo| + |hi|) + 3·2⁻¹⁰⁷⁴)); RN is monotone, so RN(w − T) > B proves w − T > B and the peak, and
# RN(w − T) < −B proves w < θ. Otherwise (|w − T| ≤ B, or T = ±∞ past DBL_MAX) _km_peak_above_exact decides.
#must_use
fn _km_find_peaks_run(data, n, t: f64, lo: f64, hi: f64, out, room, force): (i64, i64) {
    var omt: f64 = 1.0 - t;
    var thr: f64 = (lo * omt) + (hi * t);  # Rust's interpolated threshold
    var c51: f64 = 0x3CC0000000000000;      # 2⁻⁵¹
    var alo: f64 = f64_abs(lo);
    var ahi: f64 = f64_abs(hi);
    var b1: f64 = alo * c51;
    var b2: f64 = ahi * c51;
    var m1022: f64 = 0x0010000000000000;    # 2⁻¹⁰²²
    var p50: f64 = 0x3CD0000000000000;      # 2⁻⁵⁰
    var tiny: f64 = m1022 * p50;            # 2⁻¹⁰⁷², exactly
    var bnd: f64 = (b1 + b2) + tiny;
    var tfin = _km_is_finite(thr);
    if (force == 1) { tfin = 0; }
    var count_first = 0;
    if (room < (n - 1) / 2) { count_first = 1; }
    var pass = count_first;                 # pass 1 counts (only when the buffer might be too small), pass 0 writes
    var slow = 0;
    while (pass >= 0) {
        var cnt = 0;
        slow = 0;
        var i = 1;
        while (i < n - 1) {
            var w0: f64 = load64(data + 8 * (i - 1));
            var w1: f64 = load64(data + 8 * i);
            var w2: f64 = load64(data + 8 * (i + 1));
            if (w1 > w0 && w1 > w2) {
                var above = 0;
                var dw: f64 = w1 - w1;
                if (dw != 0.0) {
                    above = 1;              # +∞ (NaN and −∞ fail the neighbour test)
                } elif (tfin == 1) {
                    var d: f64 = w1 - thr;
                    var nb: f64 = -bnd;
                    if (d > bnd) {
                        above = 1;
                    } elif (d >= nb) {
                        above = _km_peak_above_exact(w1, lo, hi, t);
                        slow = slow + 1;
                    }
                } else {
                    above = _km_peak_above_exact(w1, lo, hi, t);
                    slow = slow + 1;
                }
                if (above == 1) {
                    if (pass == 0) { store64(out + 8 * cnt, i); }
                    cnt = cnt + 1;
                }
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if (pass == 0) { return (cnt, slow); }
        if (cnt > room) { return (0 - 1, slow); }
        pass = pass - 1;
    }
    return (0, slow);
}

# Strict local maxima above a threshold: the indices i (1 ≤ i ≤ n − 2: end points are never peaks) of the n values at
# `data` with data[i] > data[i − 1], data[i] > data[i + 1] and data[i] > min + t·(max − min), min and max taken over
# the finite values (Rust's fold from (+∞, −∞) with its NaN-ignoring min / max), t = `threshold`, a fraction of the
# range. NaN comparisons are false. ADR 0002 #37 (the overflow audit's B6): the threshold test is exact — data[i] >
# min·(1 − t) + max·t in rational arithmetic with t the given double, where Rust rounds the threshold (1 − t, the two
# products and the sum, at the subnormal grid too) before comparing: [−0, 5·10⁻³²⁴, 0] at t = 0.75 has the exact
# threshold 3.7·10⁻³²⁴, so index 1 is a peak (Rust's rounds to 5·10⁻³²⁴: none), and [10, 10.2, 10, 11, 10] at t = 1 −
# 2⁻⁵³ the exact 11 − 2⁻⁵³ (Rust's 11), so index 3 is one. Rust's threshold decides wherever a proven bound separates
# the value from it (_km_find_peaks_run). The indices, in ascending order, go to the caller's buffer `out` of `cap` i64
# words: Ok(count); Ok(0) when n < 3, t is not in [0, 1] (NaN included) or no value is finite, as Rust's empty Vec.
# Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT) if (data, n) is no slice, if `out` starts inside it past its first value (data < out <
# data + 8·n, checked after Rust's three Ok(0) cases, which write nothing: the indices would overwrite values still
# to be scanned; out = data, in place, and out < data work, since the k-th index is written only after value 2k + 2
# has been read), or if more peaks are found than cap holds (`out` untouched; at most (n − 1)/2 can be). Mirrors
# `spectroscopy::find_peaks`.
#must_use
fn find_peaks(data, n, threshold: f64, out, cap): Result {
    if (_km_is_slice(data, n) != 1) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var in_range = 0;                       # (0.0..=1.0).contains(&threshold): false for NaN
    if (threshold >= 0.0 && threshold <= 1.0) { in_range = 1; }
    if (n < 3 || in_range != 1) { return Ok(0); }
    var lo: f64 = _KM_INF;
    var hi: f64 = -lo;
    var k = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        var v: f64 = load64(data + 8 * k);
        # _km_is_finite, _km_min and _km_max inline: v − v is 0 exactly for a finite v, and lo and hi are never NaN.
        var dv: f64 = v - v;
        if (dv == 0.0) {
            if (!(lo < v)) { lo = v; }
            if (!(hi > v)) { hi = v; }
        }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (lo > hi) { return Ok(0); }          # no finite values
    if (out > data && out < data + 8 * n) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    var room = cap;
    if (out == 0 || room < 0) { room = 0; }
    var cnt, slow = _km_find_peaks_run(data, n, threshold, lo, hi, out, room, 0);
    if (cnt < 0) { return Err(KM_ERR_INVALID_INPUT); }
    return Ok(cnt);
}
